WEBVTT

1
00:00:19,144 --> 00:00:22,189
[pickaxe thudding]

2
00:00:32,449 --> 00:00:34,743
[equipment rattles]

3
00:00:41,291 --> 00:00:43,126
[something opens]

4
00:00:52,469 --> 00:00:55,472
[film projector whirring]

5
00:00:56,682 --> 00:00:59,142
[♪ gentle music playing]

6
00:01:09,069 --> 00:01:13,073
[♪ gentle music playing]

7
00:01:19,246 --> 00:01:21,081
[microphone tapping]

8
00:01:22,499 --> 00:01:23,834
[Andri] Hello.

9
00:01:25,711 --> 00:01:27,129
Hello.

10
00:01:28,505 --> 00:01:29,756
Hello.

11
00:01:33,969 --> 00:01:35,971
I hope that this will reach you.

12
00:01:39,891 --> 00:01:43,353
We will never meet because
we live in different times.

13
00:01:51,194 --> 00:01:54,031
I cannot send you a glacier,

14
00:01:54,906 --> 00:01:56,533
but at least I
can send you this.

15
00:02:13,300 --> 00:02:17,679
[♪ theme music playing]

16
00:02:37,240 --> 00:02:39,242
[projector whirring]

17
00:02:42,204 --> 00:02:44,288
[jungle birdsong]

18
00:02:44,289 --> 00:02:46,249
[water lapping]

19
00:02:49,169 --> 00:02:51,380
[insects chirping]

20
00:02:52,339 --> 00:02:54,757
This capsule could have
been filled with snakes

21
00:02:54,758 --> 00:02:56,051
from the Amazon.

22
00:03:01,431 --> 00:03:04,308
This boa constrictor on
my shoulders belongs to my

23
00:03:04,309 --> 00:03:05,894
favorite uncle, John.

24
00:03:07,896 --> 00:03:09,188
15 years later,

25
00:03:09,189 --> 00:03:11,857
he invites me to help him
research the mating habits

26
00:03:11,858 --> 00:03:14,403
of the giant anaconda
in Venezuela.

27
00:03:17,614 --> 00:03:19,616
I would have been here,

28
00:03:20,867 --> 00:03:23,829
but these plans conflict
with my own mating habits.

29
00:03:24,996 --> 00:03:26,790
{\an8}[baby exclaims]

30
00:03:27,707 --> 00:03:30,210
{\an8}So I stay in my own
habitat here in Iceland,

31
00:03:31,128 --> 00:03:32,921
{\an8}writing poetry and
science fiction.

32
00:03:34,297 --> 00:03:35,714
[host] Andri
Snaer Magnason's book,

33
00:03:35,715 --> 00:03:37,425
"The Story of the Blue Planet"

34
00:03:37,426 --> 00:03:40,470
has garnered
significant attention.

35
00:03:40,929 --> 00:03:43,305
This is perhaps the most
political Icelandic novel I've

36
00:03:43,306 --> 00:03:45,225
read in a long time.

37
00:03:48,228 --> 00:03:49,312
[Andri] Hulda!

38
00:03:50,480 --> 00:03:52,607
Over there is the
glacier tongue, look.

39
00:03:53,150 --> 00:03:54,276
Whoops!

40
00:03:55,318 --> 00:03:57,612
Over there is the glacier.

41
00:03:59,406 --> 00:04:00,991
[Elin] Over there!

42
00:04:02,451 --> 00:04:05,494
[Andri] The first thing I'm
sending you is a story that I

43
00:04:05,495 --> 00:04:07,330
used to tell my own kids.

44
00:04:10,250 --> 00:04:12,252
It's about our home.

45
00:04:16,756 --> 00:04:20,009
[♪ stirring music playing]

46
00:04:20,010 --> 00:04:22,387
First, there was the void.

47
00:04:26,725 --> 00:04:28,769
[asteroid whooshing]

48
00:04:29,811 --> 00:04:32,147
Then, fire met the ice.

49
00:04:33,273 --> 00:04:35,066
[explosion booms!]

50
00:04:36,359 --> 00:04:37,485
[water rushing]

51
00:04:37,486 --> 00:04:42,699
In the chaos rose Auohumla,
a cow made of frost and snow.

52
00:04:48,747 --> 00:04:50,999
From her body came
hundreds of glaciers...

53
00:04:54,544 --> 00:04:57,005
...that carved
mountains and valleys.

54
00:05:03,094 --> 00:05:06,598
From her udders ran four
rivers that nourished the world.

55
00:05:12,687 --> 00:05:15,524
Her ice made a home
here in Iceland,

56
00:05:16,775 --> 00:05:19,152
and Iceland became a
home for all of us.

57
00:05:20,320 --> 00:05:22,239
[birds chirping]

58
00:05:27,160 --> 00:05:28,160
[waves crashing]

59
00:05:28,161 --> 00:05:29,704
[bright ethereal music]

60
00:05:41,424 --> 00:05:43,510
[dog barking]

61
00:05:55,564 --> 00:05:56,605
[children chattering]

62
00:05:56,606 --> 00:05:58,441
[♪ bright music playing]

63
00:05:59,359 --> 00:06:02,571
We have lived with
the ice for 1,000 years.

64
00:06:09,661 --> 00:06:10,954
[Andri] Smile!

65
00:06:14,291 --> 00:06:17,167
'Isgland'... Isgland?

66
00:06:17,168 --> 00:06:18,127
Is this correct?

67
00:06:18,128 --> 00:06:18,794
[Eilin] Yes.

68
00:06:18,795 --> 00:06:20,088
[Andri] Good job.

69
00:06:20,547 --> 00:06:21,923
[Eilin] Iceland.

70
00:06:23,550 --> 00:06:25,302
[Andri] But that was
just the beginning,

71
00:06:26,720 --> 00:06:28,388
and you will need many stories.

72
00:06:36,396 --> 00:06:38,564
[Andri] I can capture
the Northern Lights!

73
00:06:38,565 --> 00:06:39,900
Wow!

74
00:06:41,902 --> 00:06:43,820
Look, they're moving.

75
00:06:45,697 --> 00:06:46,448
Wow!

76
00:06:47,032 --> 00:06:48,824
They disappeared - whoa!

77
00:06:48,825 --> 00:06:50,410
[Eilin] They're
over here now.

78
00:06:53,413 --> 00:06:56,041
[♪ gentle music playing]

79
00:06:59,544 --> 00:07:02,505
[water rushing]

80
00:07:03,798 --> 00:07:05,716
[Andri] I don't know
how old you will be when

81
00:07:05,717 --> 00:07:07,052
you receive this.

82
00:07:10,513 --> 00:07:15,101
I am 53, speaking to
you from the year 2026.

83
00:07:20,440 --> 00:07:22,692
And this is what
Iceland looks like now.

84
00:07:23,943 --> 00:07:25,861
[♪ stirring music playing]

85
00:07:25,862 --> 00:07:26,946
[lava roars]

86
00:07:27,322 --> 00:07:30,115
[Andri] I don't think I've ever
seen anything as beautiful.

87
00:07:30,116 --> 00:07:31,784
How do you like it, girls?

88
00:07:31,785 --> 00:07:33,244
[both] It's awesome.

89
00:07:34,621 --> 00:07:36,580
[♪ bright music playing]

90
00:07:36,581 --> 00:07:37,624
[water rushing]

91
00:07:41,002 --> 00:07:43,046
[Andri] This is
such a beautiful path.

92
00:07:43,797 --> 00:07:45,506
{\an8}[bird hoots]

93
00:07:45,507 --> 00:07:46,883
{\an8}[Andri hoots back]

94
00:07:48,927 --> 00:07:51,054
- Andri, are you with us?
- Yes.

95
00:07:52,222 --> 00:07:53,390
[birds screech]

96
00:07:53,723 --> 00:07:55,225
[♪ bright music playing]

97
00:07:59,479 --> 00:08:02,816
[Andri] My love for this place
comes from those who raised me,

98
00:08:05,902 --> 00:08:09,155
my parents and my grandparents.

99
00:08:15,203 --> 00:08:17,122
{\an8}We are a family of
firsts and lasts.

100
00:08:19,916 --> 00:08:23,627
{\an8}Grandpa Jon was the last
in a 1,000-year line to be

101
00:08:23,628 --> 00:08:24,796
{\an8}born on this farm.

102
00:08:27,173 --> 00:08:30,218
{\an8}Grandma Disa was
the first to laugh always.

103
00:08:31,553 --> 00:08:33,429
{\an8}[laughter]

104
00:08:33,430 --> 00:08:35,013
{\an8}[overlapping chatter]

105
00:08:35,014 --> 00:08:36,808
{\an8}And Grandma Hulda,

106
00:08:37,308 --> 00:08:39,310
{\an8}the first woman
to fly in Iceland.

107
00:08:43,064 --> 00:08:44,149
Grandpa Arni,

108
00:08:45,358 --> 00:08:47,902
one of the first
to explore Iceland's glaciers.

109
00:08:59,372 --> 00:09:02,834
But I became the first
to say goodbye to something

110
00:09:04,252 --> 00:09:06,296
we never thought
we could lose.

111
00:09:14,262 --> 00:09:17,390
[wind]

112
00:09:35,366 --> 00:09:39,162
[wind]

113
00:09:47,295 --> 00:09:50,173
[overlapping chatter]

114
00:10:02,393 --> 00:10:05,271
[singing in foreign language]

115
00:10:18,368 --> 00:10:20,328
[Andri] Now, it's 2019.

116
00:10:21,371 --> 00:10:24,207
My youngest of four kids,
Hulda, is turning 11.

117
00:10:26,501 --> 00:10:27,710
[Andri] Wow!

118
00:10:28,962 --> 00:10:30,630
[overlapping chatter]

119
00:10:31,631 --> 00:10:33,132
[Andri] And my
last grandparent,

120
00:10:33,133 --> 00:10:35,468
also named Hulda,
is still alive.

121
00:10:37,887 --> 00:10:40,932
[Andri] You are living
up to high expectations, Hulda.

122
00:10:41,641 --> 00:10:44,102
[Hulda] We Huldas here.

123
00:10:46,855 --> 00:10:49,566
[camera shutter clicks]

124
00:10:56,322 --> 00:10:59,408
[Andri] Grandma Hulda had been
married to my grandfather Arni

125
00:10:59,409 --> 00:11:01,161
for 60 years.

126
00:11:05,665 --> 00:11:09,042
Now, the things they
love most in the world are

127
00:11:09,043 --> 00:11:10,336
beginning to die.

128
00:11:24,767 --> 00:11:28,145
[reporter] In 2014, Ok
Glacier was the first glacier to

129
00:11:28,146 --> 00:11:31,356
die due to climate change.

130
00:11:31,357 --> 00:11:35,235
Now, a funeral commemorating
it will be held in August.

131
00:11:35,236 --> 00:11:39,532
This funeral is the first
of its kind in the world.

132
00:11:42,076 --> 00:11:44,996
[Andri] A funeral for a
glacier feels surreal,

133
00:11:47,290 --> 00:11:49,709
like my sense of home
is slipping away.

134
00:11:52,170 --> 00:11:54,922
I had been collecting my
grandparents' glacier stories

135
00:11:54,923 --> 00:11:56,382
since I could remember.

136
00:11:58,801 --> 00:12:01,387
And now, suddenly, I
see them in a new light.

137
00:12:12,815 --> 00:12:15,360
I have this urge to
gather up all of them,

138
00:12:16,277 --> 00:12:18,821
thinking they may be
useful for the future.

139
00:12:26,913 --> 00:12:28,623
Let me send you
my favorite one,

140
00:12:29,624 --> 00:12:32,961
how my grandparents
fell in love on the ice.

141
00:12:41,010 --> 00:12:42,679
This is their footage.

142
00:12:51,521 --> 00:12:54,983
Here they are on their
honeymoon in 1956.

143
00:12:57,944 --> 00:12:59,821
{\an8}And here they are in 2004,

144
00:13:01,239 --> 00:13:05,368
2010, and 2017.

145
00:13:10,957 --> 00:13:13,418
[Hulda] From a young age,

146
00:13:14,502 --> 00:13:17,297
I just craved going
to the mountains.

147
00:13:20,008 --> 00:13:23,468
In the springtime,
strangely enough,

148
00:13:23,469 --> 00:13:26,723
I start to smell
a glacier scent.

149
00:13:30,351 --> 00:13:32,186
[Andri] What
is a “glacier scent?”

150
00:13:32,645 --> 00:13:34,313
[Hulda] Well,
I can't describe it.

151
00:13:34,314 --> 00:13:36,816
- It was something
special.

152
00:13:37,692 --> 00:13:42,113
There was just this smell -
it was exhilarating.

153
00:13:44,699 --> 00:13:45,950
[Andri] And
when did you meet?

154
00:13:46,909 --> 00:13:49,620
[Hulda] It was a
meeting of mountaineers.

155
00:13:51,581 --> 00:13:54,292
[Arni] I liked
her right away.

156
00:13:56,878 --> 00:13:59,463
[Hulda] I used
to say to my mom,

157
00:13:59,464 --> 00:14:03,468
"Oh, I wish I was a boy,
then I could do this and that."

158
00:14:04,594 --> 00:14:07,263
To have more adventures.

159
00:14:08,181 --> 00:14:11,350
I was just up for anything.

160
00:14:13,686 --> 00:14:18,691
Arni says to me, "We are going
up to the Vatnajokull glacier."

161
00:14:19,025 --> 00:14:21,736
And I say, "Listen,
can I go with you?"

162
00:14:23,071 --> 00:14:27,324
[Arni] The local
skiers asked if I was crazy,

163
00:14:27,325 --> 00:14:30,161
taking a woman up
to Vatnajokull.

164
00:14:30,495 --> 00:14:33,164
That’s completely ridiculous!

165
00:14:40,713 --> 00:14:43,507
[host] The largest
glacier is Vatnajokull,

166
00:14:43,508 --> 00:14:46,134
which covers about one twelfth
of Iceland's surface

167
00:14:46,135 --> 00:14:48,178
and is the largest
glacier in Europe.

168
00:14:48,179 --> 00:14:51,681
Trips up to
Vatnajokull are an act of daring

169
00:14:51,682 --> 00:14:56,896
only for well-equipped and
highly experienced mountaineers.

170
00:15:06,531 --> 00:15:09,617
[Hulda] We spent a
whole week on the glacier.

171
00:15:11,160 --> 00:15:13,204
[Arni] I don’t think
we have parted since.

172
00:15:19,752 --> 00:15:22,170
[Hulda] We got
married on Friday,

173
00:15:22,171 --> 00:15:27,552
and left early the next
morning for a three-week trip.

174
00:15:42,400 --> 00:15:46,237
After that, we often went
up to Vatnajokull Glacier

175
00:15:46,779 --> 00:15:49,448
with the Icelandic
Glaciological Society.

176
00:15:53,369 --> 00:15:55,413
[Arni] This was the
beginning of the surveying.

177
00:16:00,960 --> 00:16:04,964
These are the same measurements
they are taking today.

178
00:16:07,466 --> 00:16:09,844
[gusting wind]

179
00:16:14,724 --> 00:16:15,975
[Arni] Were you ever cold?

180
00:16:17,185 --> 00:16:18,811
[Hulda] No, never cold.

181
00:16:19,770 --> 00:16:22,190
We were newlyweds!

182
00:16:25,610 --> 00:16:28,279
[Andri] Do you consider the
glacier to be your second home?

183
00:16:28,863 --> 00:16:30,489
[Hulda] I do.

184
00:16:35,703 --> 00:16:39,582
It’s a different world
being on top of a glacier.

185
00:16:43,252 --> 00:16:45,213
Everything is white.

186
00:16:49,091 --> 00:16:51,135
It's just endless.

187
00:17:03,105 --> 00:17:04,982
[film reel unspools]

188
00:17:07,235 --> 00:17:09,070
[Andri] How
old are you now?

189
00:17:10,947 --> 00:17:12,322
- I've forgotten
everything.

190
00:17:12,323 --> 00:17:14,908
- You're
turning 95, my Arni.

191
00:17:14,909 --> 00:17:16,368
- 95?
- Yes.

192
00:17:16,369 --> 00:17:19,120
[Arni] Yes, you
keep track of this for me.

193
00:17:19,121 --> 00:17:21,081
[Hulda] Yes, yes, I do.

194
00:17:21,082 --> 00:17:22,832
[Arni] Completely.

195
00:17:22,833 --> 00:17:25,794
And you always do.

196
00:17:25,795 --> 00:17:29,422
I don't know how I would have
gone through life

197
00:17:29,423 --> 00:17:33,970
if I hadn't had her with me.

198
00:17:41,143 --> 00:17:44,355
[Andri] I want you to know
glaciers as my grandparents did.

199
00:17:45,481 --> 00:17:48,401
[wind]

200
00:17:49,735 --> 00:17:51,362
As I do.

201
00:17:53,364 --> 00:17:56,367
[wind]

202
00:18:06,752 --> 00:18:10,172
[distant wind]

203
00:18:20,099 --> 00:18:22,101
[wind and microscopic debris]

204
00:18:28,232 --> 00:18:31,402
[gusting winds]

205
00:18:38,618 --> 00:18:42,121
[blizzard wind]

206
00:18:54,884 --> 00:18:58,429
[ice creaking]

207
00:19:08,981 --> 00:19:12,735
[ice creaking]

208
00:19:25,831 --> 00:19:28,334
[ice creaking]

209
00:19:29,835 --> 00:19:31,879
This is how a glacier sounds.

210
00:19:33,881 --> 00:19:36,425
You can't quite see it,
but it's moving.

211
00:19:41,180 --> 00:19:46,102
[ice creaking]

212
00:19:56,237 --> 00:20:01,032
[ice creaking]

213
00:20:01,033 --> 00:20:04,036
When snowfall stacks up long
enough, year after year,

214
00:20:05,663 --> 00:20:08,958
the ice grows heavy and starts
to move under its own weight.

215
00:20:14,630 --> 00:20:17,508
That movement is what
defines a glacier,

216
00:20:18,551 --> 00:20:20,886
ice that has come alive.

217
00:20:27,017 --> 00:20:32,815
[ice rumbling]

218
00:20:39,655 --> 00:20:42,700
A glacier's life unfolds
in geologic time,

219
00:20:44,076 --> 00:20:47,121
over centuries and millennia
instead of months and years.

220
00:20:54,837 --> 00:20:57,381
Blue is the color
of the aged ice.

221
00:21:00,468 --> 00:21:03,262
The deeper the blue,
the deeper the time.

222
00:21:15,149 --> 00:21:18,277
As long as winter snowfall
exceeds summer melt,

223
00:21:19,570 --> 00:21:21,655
the glacier will keep flowing.

224
00:21:29,163 --> 00:21:31,123
{\an8}This cycle brings
life to the land.

225
00:21:33,417 --> 00:21:34,793
[♪ gentle music playing]

226
00:21:34,794 --> 00:21:36,504
[birds chirping]

227
00:21:44,678 --> 00:21:47,640
[water rushing]

228
00:21:55,105 --> 00:21:57,525
My grandparents thought
glaciers would always be there.

229
00:22:03,239 --> 00:22:05,032
I had thought this too.

230
00:22:14,083 --> 00:22:18,087
But then one day in 2014,
this is on the news.

231
00:22:21,465 --> 00:22:25,343
[host] Ok Glacier was on
a mountain in Western Iceland.

232
00:22:25,344 --> 00:22:28,514
It was always known as
Iceland's smallest glacier

233
00:22:29,223 --> 00:22:32,725
but it will not be considered
a glacier anymore

234
00:22:32,726 --> 00:22:35,144
because glaciologist
Oddur Sigurosson is on his way

235
00:22:35,145 --> 00:22:36,396
up the mountain

236
00:22:36,397 --> 00:22:39,608
to issue a death
certificate for the glacier.

237
00:22:41,944 --> 00:22:46,949
[Oddur] I put down the cause of
death as extreme summer heat.

238
00:22:48,200 --> 00:22:52,246
Ok Glacier was only about
700 years old at its demise.

239
00:22:52,371 --> 00:22:55,249
That is to say, a mere
infant among glaciers.

240
00:22:55,875 --> 00:23:01,254
Around the year 1900 it was
about 15 square kilometers.

241
00:23:01,255 --> 00:23:04,007
But in the last 15 years,

242
00:23:04,008 --> 00:23:07,761
it has shrunk faster than I
imagined glaciers could shrink.

243
00:23:09,388 --> 00:23:13,684
It has declined remarkably fast.

244
00:23:14,768 --> 00:23:20,190
It doesn’t move anymore
and that's why it is "dead ice."

245
00:23:29,074 --> 00:23:32,244
[water trickling]

246
00:23:33,704 --> 00:23:36,414
[Andri] I wonder if the death
of a glacier will seem as

247
00:23:36,415 --> 00:23:38,834
historic to you
as it does to me.

248
00:23:40,920 --> 00:23:42,254
[water trickling]

249
00:23:42,922 --> 00:23:45,591
For me, this is when
the horizon collapsed.

250
00:23:49,178 --> 00:23:52,014
The future we were warned
about is no longer distant;

251
00:23:54,433 --> 00:23:56,310
it is here.

252
00:24:04,652 --> 00:24:06,820
Time is running out.

253
00:24:14,578 --> 00:24:18,082
The first time I felt this way,
I picked up a camera.

254
00:24:19,792 --> 00:24:21,250
And, without knowing it,

255
00:24:21,251 --> 00:24:23,504
began making this time
capsule for you.

256
00:24:32,888 --> 00:24:37,183
It's 1997, my girlfriend Magga
has just given birth to our

257
00:24:37,184 --> 00:24:38,978
first child, Hlynur.

258
00:24:40,854 --> 00:24:43,232
[Andri] This is Hlynur's
first bath

259
00:24:43,565 --> 00:24:45,359
with his mother.

260
00:24:45,776 --> 00:24:47,695
Isn't this beautiful?

261
00:24:53,534 --> 00:24:56,244
[Andri] At the same time,
my other grandfather, Jon,

262
00:24:56,245 --> 00:24:58,080
tells us he may be dying.

263
00:25:00,499 --> 00:25:02,626
So we all come running
to his seaside home.

264
00:25:06,880 --> 00:25:10,008
[Andri] Here we have four
generations lying on the grass.

265
00:25:10,009 --> 00:25:12,386
The heirs to the throne.

266
00:25:12,511 --> 00:25:14,846
The oldest man, with his
oldest son, and his oldest son,

267
00:25:14,847 --> 00:25:16,889
and his oldest son.

268
00:25:16,890 --> 00:25:20,227
- The oldest of them all.

269
00:25:23,939 --> 00:25:25,566
[Andri] Look how cool he is.

270
00:25:28,610 --> 00:25:30,571
I'm not ready to say goodbye.

271
00:25:39,538 --> 00:25:42,833
Earlier that summer, I take a
job at the National Archives,

272
00:25:44,168 --> 00:25:47,046
a place that preserves the
oldest records in Iceland.

273
00:25:49,840 --> 00:25:53,844
Each archive is a portal that
sends me to another time.

274
00:25:56,138 --> 00:25:58,265
I travel through
the Eddas to 1220,

275
00:25:59,016 --> 00:26:02,561
when our myths of gods and the
cosmos are first transcribed.

276
00:26:06,148 --> 00:26:09,026
The Book of Settlements
takes me to 874,

277
00:26:09,860 --> 00:26:11,737
when the first humans
settled in Iceland.

278
00:26:15,199 --> 00:26:19,078
Now, I'm in 1862, in a
folktale about elves.

279
00:26:20,662 --> 00:26:23,623
Here, an elf has lured
a shepherd into her home

280
00:26:23,624 --> 00:26:25,042
inside the rocks.

281
00:26:30,297 --> 00:26:34,760
Then, I land in the living room
of an old woman born in 1918.

282
00:26:36,303 --> 00:26:38,889
She is singing one of the
oldest art forms in Iceland,

283
00:26:39,807 --> 00:26:43,519
song poems called rimur,
the Icelandic word for rhyme.

284
00:26:53,320 --> 00:26:58,534
[singing in foreign language]

285
00:27:07,334 --> 00:27:12,631
[singing in foreign language]

286
00:27:23,225 --> 00:27:26,186
[singing in foreign language]

287
00:27:30,899 --> 00:27:34,570
[Andri] Rimur feels like such a
discovery that I share it on TV.

288
00:27:35,779 --> 00:27:38,447
- I had never
heard anything like it.

289
00:27:38,448 --> 00:27:41,033
But when I started listening
carefully to the lyrics,

290
00:27:41,034 --> 00:27:44,746
it contained such sorrow.

291
00:27:45,414 --> 00:27:46,747
It was...

292
00:27:46,748 --> 00:27:50,127
about the collapse
of the old ways.

293
00:27:50,252 --> 00:27:56,424
As I listened to the
constant repetition and rhythm,

294
00:27:56,425 --> 00:27:58,552
I became enchanted by it.

295
00:27:59,344 --> 00:28:01,429
[Andri whistling Rimur tune]

296
00:28:01,430 --> 00:28:03,682
[Andri] I play the recordings
for my son when he gets fussy.

297
00:28:05,017 --> 00:28:07,185
He calms down as soon as
the melodies start up.

298
00:28:09,479 --> 00:28:13,608
This old woman singing my son
to sleep revives a world lost

299
00:28:13,609 --> 00:28:15,694
to me, until now.

300
00:28:20,991 --> 00:28:23,744
[Andri] We're filming him
now, when he can't do anything,

301
00:28:24,328 --> 00:28:26,413
and when he starts
talking and being fun,

302
00:28:27,581 --> 00:28:29,833
we won't take any
photos of him at all.

303
00:28:30,792 --> 00:28:33,587
But I'll always have this.

304
00:28:37,925 --> 00:28:40,092
[overlapping chatter]

305
00:28:40,093 --> 00:28:42,763
[Andri] I was never a proper
nerd with a proper camera.

306
00:28:43,764 --> 00:28:46,766
But thinking of
Grandpa Jon's last summer,

307
00:28:46,767 --> 00:28:49,436
I just grab what is
handy and hit record.

308
00:28:51,021 --> 00:28:53,230
- Andri, is there a
zoom lens on that thing?

309
00:28:53,231 --> 00:28:54,899
- Yeah.

310
00:28:54,900 --> 00:28:57,027
- Are you sure?
- Yeah.

311
00:28:59,529 --> 00:29:02,032
[Andri] When you look
back over the last 50 years...

312
00:29:02,491 --> 00:29:03,908
what stands out?

313
00:29:03,909 --> 00:29:05,202
[Jon] What?

314
00:29:07,496 --> 00:29:09,831
It's how fun it is
to make mistakes.

315
00:29:10,332 --> 00:29:13,085
[Andri] It's fun to make
mistakes? - Yeah.

316
00:29:13,669 --> 00:29:15,336
That stands out.

317
00:29:15,337 --> 00:29:17,547
That stands out?
- Yeah.

318
00:29:19,341 --> 00:29:21,467
And it never pays to save money.

319
00:29:21,468 --> 00:29:22,760
- It never
pays to save money.

320
00:29:22,761 --> 00:29:24,054
No.
- No.

321
00:29:26,390 --> 00:29:28,391
[Andri] The more I film,
the more I can hold on to

322
00:29:28,392 --> 00:29:30,102
Grandpa's every word.

323
00:29:32,688 --> 00:29:35,607
It's as if recording him can
transport him into the future,

324
00:29:36,608 --> 00:29:38,485
where I can meet him once again.

325
00:29:39,236 --> 00:29:43,572
[Jon] It was the loveliest
proposal and confession

326
00:29:43,573 --> 00:29:46,367
I've ever
heard from a woman.

327
00:29:46,368 --> 00:29:47,660
[Disa] Is that so?

328
00:29:47,661 --> 00:29:50,997
[Jon] No Icelandic woman has
ever said anything half as
beautiful.

329
00:29:50,998 --> 00:29:52,456
[Andri] What did she say?

330
00:29:52,457 --> 00:29:54,166
[Jon] You want
to know what she said?

331
00:29:54,167 --> 00:29:57,504
[Disa] That's a secret.

332
00:30:00,215 --> 00:30:02,925
[Jon] The loveliest
proposal I've ever heard.

333
00:30:02,926 --> 00:30:05,594
It's a secret.
- It's a secret.

334
00:30:05,595 --> 00:30:08,305
[Jon] But we should
definitely tell Andri.

335
00:30:08,306 --> 00:30:09,725
[Disa] No!

336
00:30:10,934 --> 00:30:12,602
[laughter]

337
00:30:17,441 --> 00:30:18,859
[laughter]

338
00:30:21,445 --> 00:30:22,695
[Andri] From this
point forward,

339
00:30:22,696 --> 00:30:26,408
I keep the camera rolling and
record everything that I love.

340
00:30:34,249 --> 00:30:36,418
[Andri] Everything's
covered in flowers.

341
00:30:36,752 --> 00:30:38,962
Petur Jonsson's gymnastics show.

342
00:30:39,838 --> 00:30:42,173
That's Bosi, he's
going swimming.

343
00:30:42,174 --> 00:30:43,675
It's approaching midnight.

344
00:30:45,844 --> 00:30:46,970
Grandma!

345
00:30:47,763 --> 00:30:49,013
They're playing dodgeball.

346
00:30:49,014 --> 00:30:50,014
A can of whipped cream.

347
00:30:50,015 --> 00:30:50,890
The beautiful cake.

348
00:30:50,891 --> 00:30:51,932
It's 11 o'clock.

349
00:30:51,933 --> 00:30:53,185
Time for the boot toss.

350
00:30:56,646 --> 00:30:59,024
Mmm, the steak is ready.

351
00:30:59,858 --> 00:31:01,026
Hello Magga.
- Hi.

352
00:31:01,485 --> 00:31:03,069
[Andri] Hello Hlynur.
- Hi.

353
00:31:03,070 --> 00:31:05,489
How old are you now?
- Four.

354
00:31:05,947 --> 00:31:07,740
[Andri] How
old are you, Hlynur?

355
00:31:07,741 --> 00:31:08,783
[Hlynur] Five.

356
00:31:08,784 --> 00:31:09,950
Six years old.

357
00:31:09,951 --> 00:31:11,243
[Andri]
Hey Kristín Lovisa.

358
00:31:11,244 --> 00:31:12,204
Are you turning six months old?

359
00:31:12,579 --> 00:31:13,954
Hulda Filippia,

360
00:31:13,955 --> 00:31:15,373
are you one year old today?

361
00:31:15,791 --> 00:31:18,084
Elin Freyja, how old are you?

362
00:31:18,085 --> 00:31:19,169
- Two years old.

363
00:31:19,961 --> 00:31:21,671
[Kristin] Now I am three.

364
00:31:22,172 --> 00:31:23,756
[Andri] Have
you gone nuts?

365
00:31:23,757 --> 00:31:25,884
[Kristin] I
am a wild child!

366
00:31:28,678 --> 00:31:30,346
[Hlynur] I'm
only one year old.

367
00:31:30,347 --> 00:31:31,890
[Magga] Oh
my, the tiny baby.

368
00:31:32,557 --> 00:31:34,767
[Andri] Magga, look
how young you are now.

369
00:31:34,768 --> 00:31:36,185
Only 30 years old.

370
00:31:36,186 --> 00:31:38,563
- Aw, 31.

371
00:31:39,106 --> 00:31:40,982
[Andri] And how old am I?

372
00:31:42,067 --> 00:31:44,319
[Hlynur] You're
five years old!

373
00:31:51,576 --> 00:31:54,204
[laughter]

374
00:32:08,218 --> 00:32:10,637
[laughter]

375
00:32:24,568 --> 00:32:27,821
[Andri] In our family, Grandpa
Arni was our first archivist.

376
00:32:37,956 --> 00:32:42,544
[♪ gentle music playing]

377
00:32:47,549 --> 00:32:50,468
Through his records, I can
step into his memories.

378
00:33:06,693 --> 00:33:08,903
And so many of his
memories transport me to the

379
00:33:08,904 --> 00:33:10,447
place he loves most.

380
00:33:15,285 --> 00:33:18,079
[Andri] You took such
special photos on the glaciers.

381
00:33:18,705 --> 00:33:21,208
[Arni] Yeah, I did...

382
00:33:23,710 --> 00:33:25,836
[Andri] When I was a kid,
Grandpa Arni would put on

383
00:33:25,837 --> 00:33:29,007
slideshows and tell us
tales of adventures.

384
00:33:45,815 --> 00:33:48,318
Each photo jogs Arni's memory.

385
00:33:54,241 --> 00:33:57,243
With this slide, he recalls
Hulda's happiness as the ice

386
00:33:57,244 --> 00:33:58,828
mingles with the clouds.

387
00:34:04,751 --> 00:34:08,046
With this one, he remembers
places waiting to be named.

388
00:34:12,050 --> 00:34:14,552
The glacier plays tricks
on his memory too.

389
00:34:18,431 --> 00:34:20,976
"That's how it is on a glacier,"
Grandpa Arni says.

390
00:34:21,893 --> 00:34:24,853
"You think you've walked
this path before, but then,

391
00:34:24,854 --> 00:34:27,983
all of a sudden,
there's a crevice."

392
00:34:39,536 --> 00:34:46,167
[slide projector clacking]

393
00:34:48,378 --> 00:34:53,507
And every once in a while, a
dahlia from his garden bursts

394
00:34:53,508 --> 00:34:55,677
into the picture.

395
00:35:03,184 --> 00:35:07,230
With this slide, Arni talks
about a certain kind of peace.

396
00:35:10,150 --> 00:35:12,109
All these years later,

397
00:35:12,110 --> 00:35:14,613
I understand just
what this peace means.

398
00:35:19,117 --> 00:35:20,910
[Andri] What
is your first memory?

399
00:35:22,662 --> 00:35:26,291
[Arni] I actually remember
quite well when my dad died.

400
00:35:26,750 --> 00:35:29,753
[Andri] How old
were you when he drowned?

401
00:35:30,170 --> 00:35:31,796
[Arni] 12 years old.

402
00:35:37,552 --> 00:35:39,220
It naturally...

403
00:35:40,305 --> 00:35:42,349
was a very big shock.

404
00:35:48,188 --> 00:35:50,022
[Andri] I always
thought that this memory

405
00:35:50,023 --> 00:35:53,526
pushed him away from
the ocean and far uphill.

406
00:36:00,241 --> 00:36:03,328
For Hulda, glaciers were
a place of solace too.

407
00:36:06,206 --> 00:36:08,583
[Hulda] First, I was...

408
00:36:09,292 --> 00:36:12,504
engaged to another and
had twins with him.

409
00:36:12,962 --> 00:36:15,256
Two lovely girls.

410
00:36:16,174 --> 00:36:19,803
He went to study in
the United States.

411
00:36:21,054 --> 00:36:23,765
And he found himself
an American

412
00:36:24,265 --> 00:36:26,351
and just broke up with me.

413
00:36:26,726 --> 00:36:28,937
That was naturally
a bit of a shock.

414
00:36:36,069 --> 00:36:40,407
[Andri] She found strength
in the sky and in the ice.

415
00:36:50,500 --> 00:36:54,671
[♪ gentle music playing]

416
00:37:02,887 --> 00:37:05,849
Grandpa Arni likens
photography to glaciers;

417
00:37:07,225 --> 00:37:10,353
they also record
the passage of time.

418
00:37:19,904 --> 00:37:21,614
Our glaciers contain:

419
00:37:23,241 --> 00:37:25,285
a volcanic eruption from 1918,

420
00:37:28,163 --> 00:37:30,081
another from 1983,

421
00:37:33,293 --> 00:37:34,753
one from 2004,

422
00:37:37,964 --> 00:37:39,591
and 2011.

423
00:37:55,440 --> 00:37:58,442
Bubbles in the ice hold
ancient air that tells stories

424
00:37:58,443 --> 00:38:00,278
of seasons passed.

425
00:38:01,863 --> 00:38:06,576
Their warmth, their cold,
their precipitations,

426
00:38:08,745 --> 00:38:11,414
their pollens,
their pollutants.

427
00:38:16,461 --> 00:38:18,463
My grandparents were some
of the first to touch this

428
00:38:18,588 --> 00:38:20,548
archive of deep time.

429
00:38:21,633 --> 00:38:24,177
[host] Another milestone
was reached in the research

430
00:38:24,302 --> 00:38:26,221
of Vatnajokull Glacier.

431
00:38:26,971 --> 00:38:31,434
[Hulda] They were drilling to
reach the ice core down there.

432
00:38:34,020 --> 00:38:36,438
I was an assistant...

433
00:38:36,439 --> 00:38:38,191
There I am.

434
00:38:38,691 --> 00:38:40,985
This was fantastic.

435
00:38:42,153 --> 00:38:44,781
[host] Finally, an
ice core has been obtained

436
00:38:44,906 --> 00:38:47,075
containing valuable information

437
00:38:47,200 --> 00:38:49,827
about the internal
structure of temperate glaciers

438
00:38:49,828 --> 00:38:52,412
and information on climate
changes in Iceland

439
00:38:52,413 --> 00:38:54,332
back to the year 1500.

440
00:38:56,042 --> 00:38:59,254
[Arni] That was,
and is, quite special.

441
00:39:02,966 --> 00:39:04,926
[repeated tapping]

442
00:39:06,761 --> 00:39:10,098
[Andri] Arni and Hulda would
tell me that to hold an ice core

443
00:39:10,640 --> 00:39:13,643
is to hold the Earth's
memories in your hand.

444
00:39:22,110 --> 00:39:27,156
[♪ ethereal music playing]

445
00:39:38,376 --> 00:39:42,839
[♪ ethereal music playing]

446
00:39:54,183 --> 00:39:57,478
[wind]

447
00:40:07,071 --> 00:40:09,823
[overlapping chatter]

448
00:40:09,824 --> 00:40:10,783
[Andri] Smile!

449
00:40:13,620 --> 00:40:14,746
I'm making a video.

450
00:40:15,204 --> 00:40:16,789
[Magni] Happy
birthday, champ!

451
00:40:18,875 --> 00:40:21,711
<i>It's your birthday today.</i>

452
00:40:22,337 --> 00:40:25,506
<i>That is why we sing a song.</i>

453
00:40:25,924 --> 00:40:29,843
<i>on the same day</i>

454
00:40:29,844 --> 00:40:33,014
<i>you were born</i>

455
00:40:33,431 --> 00:40:39,270
<i>Congratulations on your
lucky day, my lucky fellow.</i>

456
00:40:45,485 --> 00:40:47,904
[Andri] Grandpa Arni
won't remember this party.

457
00:40:52,450 --> 00:40:55,118
When I tell him he
had a birthday, he says,

458
00:40:55,119 --> 00:40:57,205
"How fun. How old am I now?"

459
00:41:03,836 --> 00:41:06,798
[Andri] My Grandfather,
do you remember the honeymoon?

460
00:41:07,882 --> 00:41:09,132
[Arni] Do I remember-

461
00:41:09,133 --> 00:41:11,177
[Andri] The honeymoon
up on Vatnajokull?

462
00:41:11,678 --> 00:41:14,222
[Arni] Oh no, I
don't remember that.

463
00:41:14,681 --> 00:41:17,100
[Hulda] It's ok, go ahead.

464
00:41:18,893 --> 00:41:21,396
He has become so forgetful.

465
00:41:24,941 --> 00:41:27,777
[Andri] He says it just
happened one day, like a crack;

466
00:41:30,780 --> 00:41:32,657
he couldn't remember
a single detail.

467
00:41:36,953 --> 00:41:39,497
[fireworks exploding]

468
00:41:50,133 --> 00:41:54,137
[fireworks exploding]

469
00:41:57,432 --> 00:41:59,392
[Andri] You have to kiss!

470
00:42:11,738 --> 00:42:14,407
[Andri] Now I can't blink
without a month passing me by.

471
00:42:17,994 --> 00:42:18,828
[Andri] There you go!

472
00:42:21,998 --> 00:42:24,584
Great! Great!

473
00:42:24,917 --> 00:42:27,419
[Magga] How long
did she practice? 15 minutes?

474
00:42:27,420 --> 00:42:29,422
[Andri] 15 minutes.
Oh, oh, oh...

475
00:42:29,547 --> 00:42:31,131
[Andri] It's seven o'clock

476
00:42:31,132 --> 00:42:32,758
and our thermometer shows...

477
00:42:32,759 --> 00:42:35,260
[Magga] 36. (97F)
- 36?

478
00:42:35,261 --> 00:42:38,263
[Magga] The thermometer
on the house is at 50 (122F).

479
00:42:38,264 --> 00:42:39,682
[Andri] What
country are we in?

480
00:42:42,560 --> 00:42:44,186
[rushing water]

481
00:42:44,187 --> 00:42:46,481
[wind]

482
00:42:55,031 --> 00:42:56,783
[Andri] I thought
I had more time.

483
00:43:06,250 --> 00:43:08,336
There's so much I
want to ask Arni,

484
00:43:11,297 --> 00:43:13,049
and so much more
I will never know.

485
00:43:25,311 --> 00:43:27,729
[host] Beneath the
Vatnajokull Glacier lies a

486
00:43:27,730 --> 00:43:29,190
restless family of volcanos.

487
00:43:33,820 --> 00:43:35,113
[eruption]

488
00:43:40,701 --> 00:43:43,621
[Andri] Our unpredictable
island holds many unknowns,

489
00:43:45,414 --> 00:43:49,502
like when a volcano might erupt
and unleash a glacial flood.

490
00:43:51,087 --> 00:43:54,173
[host] We see the water from the
glacier flowing down the slope.

491
00:43:54,549 --> 00:43:56,843
A formidable force.

492
00:44:02,140 --> 00:44:04,350
[Andri] We've always contended
with the elements here,

493
00:44:05,226 --> 00:44:07,061
and adjusted our
lives around them.

494
00:44:09,814 --> 00:44:14,359
[host] The flood tore massive
icebergs from the glacier’s edge

495
00:44:14,360 --> 00:44:16,654
and hurled them far
across the sands.

496
00:44:20,241 --> 00:44:23,285
Those standing here sense
how small humankind is in

497
00:44:23,286 --> 00:44:25,204
comparison to all of this.

498
00:44:36,966 --> 00:44:40,051
[Andri] Reverence for
these forces is embedded in

499
00:44:40,052 --> 00:44:41,762
the stories of my grandparents.

500
00:44:45,808 --> 00:44:48,727
Their tales reflect a way
of seeing the world where

501
00:44:48,728 --> 00:44:50,438
nature is alive...

502
00:44:53,482 --> 00:44:55,318
...and full of mysteries.

503
00:44:57,820 --> 00:45:01,114
[Hulda] Before we
changed the landscape here,

504
00:45:01,115 --> 00:45:02,283
there were these cliffs.

505
00:45:03,201 --> 00:45:06,287
I saw Hidden People
in the old days.

506
00:45:08,247 --> 00:45:12,168
We once saw a Hidden Man
suddenly appear...

507
00:45:13,127 --> 00:45:14,502
and then disappear.

508
00:45:14,503 --> 00:45:16,088
[Arni] He disappeared?
- Yes.

509
00:45:18,007 --> 00:45:20,009
[Jon] "I'm an
elf woman," she says.

510
00:45:21,010 --> 00:45:23,763
“Some of us live in cliffs..."

511
00:45:23,888 --> 00:45:28,267
"while others live
inside rocks."

512
00:45:29,727 --> 00:45:31,771
She said,

513
00:45:32,647 --> 00:45:35,483
“You can never fully see me"

514
00:45:35,816 --> 00:45:41,614
"because it takes so much of
my energy to appear to you.

515
00:45:42,740 --> 00:45:48,662
"But if I draw more energy,
I can speak to you."

516
00:45:48,663 --> 00:45:51,581
"And even influence you."

517
00:45:51,582 --> 00:45:54,335
- How are the new pants?

518
00:45:57,755 --> 00:46:00,466
[Andri] They're nice pants.
- I had them shortened.

519
00:46:00,841 --> 00:46:01,592
[Andri] Yes.

520
00:46:02,260 --> 00:46:03,552
[Disa] They were too long.

521
00:46:04,428 --> 00:46:09,392
[Jon] I cannot resist
the will of the elf woman.

522
00:46:09,767 --> 00:46:13,145
She will keep appearing
until I give in.

523
00:46:13,771 --> 00:46:18,234
For example, she
ordered me to marry Disa.

524
00:46:18,567 --> 00:46:20,319
And I did it.

525
00:46:24,699 --> 00:46:27,492
[Andri] So many of my
grandparents' generation have

526
00:46:27,493 --> 00:46:28,995
stories like this.

527
00:46:43,426 --> 00:46:45,553
Each hillock has a legend.

528
00:46:48,514 --> 00:46:50,808
Every river, a folktale.

529
00:47:00,026 --> 00:47:02,737
Every glacier, a hidden world.

530
00:47:11,871 --> 00:47:14,581
When I worked at
the National Archives,

531
00:47:14,582 --> 00:47:16,125
I found this recording,

532
00:47:17,126 --> 00:47:21,464
a farmer telling a legend of a
mythical forest inside the ice.

533
00:47:23,841 --> 00:47:31,223
[Ragnar] Hundreds of
years ago they found birch logs

534
00:47:32,099 --> 00:47:35,478
at the edge of the glacier.

535
00:47:37,855 --> 00:47:40,232
It's very strange.

536
00:47:40,775 --> 00:47:44,153
I don't understand it, but
I'm not saying it isn't true.

537
00:47:53,704 --> 00:47:55,789
[Andri] For centuries,
those living at the base of

538
00:47:55,790 --> 00:47:59,627
Vatnajokull Glacier notice
logs thawing from the ice.

539
00:48:02,171 --> 00:48:04,882
This is a strange sight in
our nearly treeless country,

540
00:48:06,300 --> 00:48:09,512
sparking rumors of a
forest inside the glacier.

541
00:48:11,889 --> 00:48:14,642
A place that hides
outlaws and trolls,

542
00:48:15,017 --> 00:48:16,769
where time stands still.

543
00:48:20,773 --> 00:48:22,650
But there is truth
to this legend.

544
00:48:24,985 --> 00:48:27,238
It's around the year 900.

545
00:48:28,030 --> 00:48:31,367
Our first ancestors just
arrived and encountered

546
00:48:31,492 --> 00:48:32,618
trees growing here.

547
00:48:34,620 --> 00:48:37,206
They name it Breioamork,
the Wide Forest.

548
00:48:39,583 --> 00:48:41,042
Over the years,

549
00:48:41,043 --> 00:48:44,588
a glacial tongue flows
down the mountain and freezes

550
00:48:45,005 --> 00:48:46,924
the trees in time.

551
00:48:54,265 --> 00:48:57,143
It's 2026 and that
tongue is still here,

552
00:48:59,103 --> 00:49:03,524
now called Breioamerkurjokull,
the Wide Forest Glacier.

553
00:49:06,402 --> 00:49:09,320
A name that ties this
living ice back to the founding

554
00:49:09,321 --> 00:49:11,073
of Iceland itself.

555
00:49:20,791 --> 00:49:26,547
[♪ gentle music playing]

556
00:49:36,265 --> 00:49:42,021
[♪ gentle music playing]

557
00:49:55,159 --> 00:49:57,203
[thuds and ice shattering]

558
00:49:58,162 --> 00:50:00,747
[host] A glaciologist
expressed concern with the

559
00:50:00,748 --> 00:50:03,666
retreat of Icelandic
glaciers in recent decades.

560
00:50:03,667 --> 00:50:06,461
He says the country will have
no glaciers in 200 years,

561
00:50:06,462 --> 00:50:08,087
possibly much sooner.

562
00:50:08,088 --> 00:50:13,093
[Oddur] Such bad news
is unprecedented in history.

563
00:50:13,802 --> 00:50:17,055
And it constantly surprises
us how it keeps receding

564
00:50:17,056 --> 00:50:20,226
faster and faster.

565
00:50:20,726 --> 00:50:23,187
This is the same phenomenon
happening in Antarctica,

566
00:50:23,312 --> 00:50:27,233
Greenland, the Himalayas and
all areas with glaciers.

567
00:50:28,025 --> 00:50:30,276
Iceland's glaciers are
1,000 years old now,

568
00:50:30,277 --> 00:50:32,530
which in some
sense means that

569
00:50:33,072 --> 00:50:37,201
we lose 5 years
of history every year.

570
00:50:40,621 --> 00:50:43,332
[Andri] I struggle with
the death of one glacier.

571
00:50:44,333 --> 00:50:47,461
And here is the news,
they all will be gone.

572
00:50:52,633 --> 00:50:55,469
Glaciers are leaving
geologic time.

573
00:50:56,220 --> 00:50:58,097
Changes that once took hundreds,

574
00:50:58,222 --> 00:51:01,975
thousands, even
millions of years are now

575
00:51:01,976 --> 00:51:05,396
unfolding in a
single human lifetime.

576
00:51:11,694 --> 00:51:15,906
[slide projector clacking]

577
00:51:20,953 --> 00:51:24,665
[slide projector clacking]

578
00:51:28,502 --> 00:51:31,963
Grandpa Arni has been
spending his days photographing

579
00:51:31,964 --> 00:51:33,966
flowers from his garden.

580
00:51:35,301 --> 00:51:38,387
[slide projector clacking]

581
00:51:39,263 --> 00:51:41,932
All he can really remember
is that he loves Hulda.

582
00:51:46,395 --> 00:51:49,481
[slide projector clacking]

583
00:51:50,482 --> 00:51:52,484
[Hulda] There's
a whole box there.

584
00:51:57,114 --> 00:51:58,699
Let's see.

585
00:51:59,783 --> 00:52:02,410
This is when we all
went to the Highlands.

586
00:52:02,411 --> 00:52:03,704
[Arni] Yes.

587
00:52:13,172 --> 00:52:15,298
[Andri] He's been
losing his balance,

588
00:52:15,299 --> 00:52:17,134
so we move him to
a care facility.

589
00:52:19,595 --> 00:52:22,139
Upon one visit, I find
him in front of the TV.

590
00:52:27,853 --> 00:52:30,856
He asks me, "Why is the
slideshow moving so fast?"

591
00:52:37,321 --> 00:52:39,865
He slowly starts to fade away.

592
00:52:49,792 --> 00:52:53,879
[♪ reflective music playing]

593
00:53:02,554 --> 00:53:07,184
[♪ reflective music playing]

594
00:53:10,479 --> 00:53:12,481
And then, he's gone.

595
00:53:24,076 --> 00:53:25,993
[Andri] Tell me your name.

596
00:53:25,994 --> 00:53:27,413
[Arni] Arni Kjartansson.

597
00:53:28,872 --> 00:53:30,540
[Andri] When were
you born?

598
00:53:30,541 --> 00:53:33,210
[Arni]
November 26th, 1922.

599
00:53:34,628 --> 00:53:36,630
[Andri] And
what day is today?

600
00:53:37,464 --> 00:53:45,264
[Arni] It’s the
27th of March, 2004.

601
00:53:47,141 --> 00:53:52,104
[Andri] We are collecting
sources and biological samples

602
00:53:52,229 --> 00:53:56,400
so you can be cloned
in the 22nd century.

603
00:54:03,449 --> 00:54:04,866
{\an8}[Andri] All these years,

604
00:54:04,867 --> 00:54:07,535
{\an8}I thought my filming
was a way to bottle each

605
00:54:07,536 --> 00:54:08,954
{\an8}and every moment.

606
00:54:12,541 --> 00:54:16,419
{\an8}But, really, I was just
watching the past flicker

607
00:54:16,420 --> 00:54:20,758
{\an8}behind glass, noticing the
effects of time on camera tape.

608
00:54:30,559 --> 00:54:32,728
Now, I see it for what it was,

609
00:54:34,646 --> 00:54:37,775
holding on to dear
life as it charges forward,

610
00:54:38,984 --> 00:54:42,154
trying to stop all that I
loved from slipping away.

611
00:54:54,583 --> 00:54:57,503
I see the impulse to
hold on everywhere.

612
00:55:32,037 --> 00:55:34,748
This is the Library of Water
in western Iceland.

613
00:55:36,250 --> 00:55:38,919
It stores melted ice
from our glaciers.

614
00:55:46,134 --> 00:55:50,931
[♪ futuristic music playing]

615
00:56:04,027 --> 00:56:09,074
[♪ futuristic music playing]

616
00:56:16,832 --> 00:56:19,084
Here is water from
Poris Glacier.

617
00:56:21,545 --> 00:56:23,797
Here is Snaefells Glacier.

618
00:56:25,424 --> 00:56:29,386
And this one is Ok Glacier,
collected just before it died.

619
00:56:36,810 --> 00:56:40,271
Perhaps, in the form of water,

620
00:56:40,272 --> 00:56:42,691
the ice could still
travel through time.

621
00:56:47,529 --> 00:56:49,698
It's the same water, after all.

622
00:56:52,909 --> 00:56:55,454
As ice, it holds memories.

623
00:56:57,539 --> 00:57:00,417
As liquid, does it lose them?

624
00:57:04,296 --> 00:57:07,716
[lighthouse softly pulsates]

625
00:57:15,724 --> 00:57:18,977
[water dripping]

626
00:57:31,740 --> 00:57:34,534
[wind blows]

627
00:57:41,750 --> 00:57:45,629
[ice and ash crumble]

628
00:57:47,506 --> 00:57:51,468
[water dripping]

629
00:57:54,846 --> 00:57:57,933
[water running]

630
00:58:01,520 --> 00:58:04,230
[♪ emotional music playing]

631
00:58:04,231 --> 00:58:08,317
I often think about
the unsettling quiet of

632
00:58:08,318 --> 00:58:09,653
a glacier's death.

633
00:58:20,872 --> 00:58:22,832
It's like a summertime

634
00:58:22,833 --> 00:58:26,002
that just came and
stayed and stayed.

635
00:58:29,589 --> 00:58:33,093
[water rushing]

636
00:58:39,850 --> 00:58:44,396
[♪ pensive music playing]

637
00:59:01,163 --> 00:59:06,418
[♪ pensive music playing]

638
00:59:09,713 --> 00:59:12,466
[waves lap on the iceberg]

639
00:59:30,734 --> 00:59:32,778
[air bubbles releasing]

640
00:59:42,412 --> 00:59:45,332
[air bubbles releasing]

641
00:59:46,792 --> 00:59:49,628
Will your oceans be
made of our glaciers?

642
00:59:55,592 --> 00:59:58,094
Your rain of our seas?

643
01:00:08,271 --> 01:00:10,232
Your water is my water.

644
01:00:14,069 --> 01:00:16,822
Your time, also mine.

645
01:00:23,703 --> 01:00:25,621
In my family,

646
01:00:25,622 --> 01:00:28,917
Grandpa Jon's horizon is
his grandfather's horizon.

647
01:00:31,795 --> 01:00:33,964
And his grandfather's
before that.

648
01:00:37,676 --> 01:00:41,346
[singing in foreign language]

649
01:00:47,352 --> 01:00:50,564
[singing in foreign language]

650
01:00:51,731 --> 01:00:54,316
[Andri] Here he is
singing "Dreamland,"

651
01:00:54,317 --> 01:00:57,445
an ode to the seaside plains
where he made his home.

652
01:01:01,700 --> 01:01:09,666
<i>...there is
the land of my dreams</i>

653
01:01:11,167 --> 01:01:13,712
[Andri] Jon's love for
his home is intertwined with

654
01:01:13,837 --> 01:01:15,547
the way he speaks about it.

655
01:01:16,673 --> 01:01:19,175
He can transport me
there with a single word.

656
01:01:23,138 --> 01:01:25,306
I think of this
one term he used...

657
01:01:25,307 --> 01:01:28,435
...fuglabjarg, the Icelandic
word for "bird cliff."

658
01:01:29,895 --> 01:01:32,981
[loud squawking]

659
01:01:43,575 --> 01:01:46,578
[loud squawking]

660
01:01:55,295 --> 01:01:58,173
It also means "a
noisy gathering."

661
01:01:59,799 --> 01:02:02,218
He used it to describe
my parents' wedding.

662
01:02:07,182 --> 01:02:09,309
It was a total
fuglabjarg in there.

663
01:02:09,935 --> 01:02:11,519
You couldn't hear
a single thing.

664
01:02:20,862 --> 01:02:24,491
But the bird cliffs that
Jon knew are no longer the

665
01:02:24,616 --> 01:02:26,326
bird cliffs that I know.

666
01:02:30,622 --> 01:02:34,209
[reporter] Seabirds
are finding it hard to adapt

667
01:02:34,334 --> 01:02:36,920
to climate change.

668
01:02:37,921 --> 01:02:40,924
Recent figures indicate that
the Icelandic puffin population

669
01:02:41,049 --> 01:02:44,094
has declined by 70% since 1995.

670
01:02:45,845 --> 01:02:47,639
Biologists say the
main reason is the rise of

671
01:02:47,764 --> 01:02:48,807
ocean temperature...

672
01:02:55,063 --> 01:02:58,692
[waves lapping]

673
01:02:59,275 --> 01:03:02,403
[Andri] Will this word
for noise, for me,

674
01:03:02,404 --> 01:03:05,073
now reference a
silent place for you?

675
01:03:09,119 --> 01:03:12,372
Maybe fuglabjarg will
become kind of a ghost word,

676
01:03:14,416 --> 01:03:17,544
where speaking it will
elicit a memory of birds who

677
01:03:17,669 --> 01:03:19,295
no longer exist.

678
01:03:25,468 --> 01:03:27,804
I wonder about the name Iceland.

679
01:03:31,516 --> 01:03:32,976
In 200 years,

680
01:03:33,101 --> 01:03:36,813
will saying my country's
name also summon a ghost?

681
01:03:50,118 --> 01:03:52,996
[overlapping chatter]

682
01:04:02,464 --> 01:04:04,214
[friend] Your
dad is videoing you.

683
01:04:04,215 --> 01:04:05,674
[Hulda] I know.

684
01:04:05,675 --> 01:04:07,176
[friend] Why?

685
01:04:07,177 --> 01:04:11,431
It's so special to see an
11-year-old girl eating cake.

686
01:04:13,600 --> 01:04:15,142
And ice cream.
- And ice cream.

687
01:04:15,143 --> 01:04:16,936
What do you think about that?

688
01:04:28,907 --> 01:04:32,035
[Andri] Shortly after
Hulda's birthday in 2019,

689
01:04:33,036 --> 01:04:35,662
I was asked to write something
to memorialize the dead

690
01:04:35,663 --> 01:04:38,583
Ok Glacier for its
upcoming funeral.

691
01:04:41,044 --> 01:04:43,754
The words would go
on a copper plaque on

692
01:04:43,755 --> 01:04:45,757
the mountain where the
glacier once lived.

693
01:04:49,177 --> 01:04:50,594
[interviewer] It's kind
of a writer's dream,

694
01:04:50,595 --> 01:04:52,263
and also a writer's nightmare.

695
01:04:52,972 --> 01:04:55,265
You've got four lines.

696
01:04:55,266 --> 01:04:57,894
How can you say something
that's going to be meaningful?

697
01:04:58,645 --> 01:05:00,188
- Yeah, and you're
writing in copper.

698
01:05:00,814 --> 01:05:05,110
So you don't have a easy delete
button if you make a mistake.

699
01:05:07,695 --> 01:05:10,782
It's like when you make a
gravestone in a cemetery,

700
01:05:11,199 --> 01:05:13,492
you, uh, somebody's
gonna read it in

701
01:05:13,493 --> 01:05:15,286
200, 300, 400, 500 years.

702
01:05:16,246 --> 01:05:19,541
Copper lasts longer than stone,
so maybe 6,000 years.

703
01:05:25,839 --> 01:05:27,674
I'm not sure how
to approach this.

704
01:05:32,220 --> 01:05:33,513
[explosion]

705
01:05:39,519 --> 01:05:41,104
Once I wrote
about the destruction

706
01:05:41,229 --> 01:05:42,814
of another landscape.

707
01:05:47,569 --> 01:05:50,737
I called upon Icelanders
to stop the construction of

708
01:05:50,738 --> 01:05:53,283
a dam that would
choke a glacial river.

709
01:06:02,250 --> 01:06:05,253
But I never thought the source
of the river itself could die.

710
01:06:07,589 --> 01:06:08,881
And this time,

711
01:06:08,882 --> 01:06:10,967
not because of a
local power company,

712
01:06:11,342 --> 01:06:13,344
but due to the world's
hunger for power.

713
01:06:22,896 --> 01:06:25,690
I have never written a
memorial for a glacier.

714
01:06:26,482 --> 01:06:28,067
No one has.

715
01:06:33,907 --> 01:06:36,450
How do you say goodbye
to what you never thought

716
01:06:36,451 --> 01:06:37,911
you could lose?

717
01:06:47,378 --> 01:06:49,129
When Grandpa Arni died,

718
01:06:49,130 --> 01:06:51,549
I could not stop
writing his obituary.

719
01:07:05,939 --> 01:07:07,607
I had too many words.

720
01:07:20,078 --> 01:07:21,621
I never felt closer to Arni.

721
01:07:23,539 --> 01:07:25,208
And yet, he's gone.

722
01:07:46,354 --> 01:07:50,233
I could see how saying
goodbye could allow his memory

723
01:07:50,358 --> 01:07:51,526
to live on.

724
01:08:03,496 --> 01:08:07,875
Grandpa Arni lived a long
life worthy of celebration.

725
01:08:12,839 --> 01:08:16,259
But Ok Glacier's life
was cut short by us;

726
01:08:19,095 --> 01:08:21,431
there's no celebrating that.

727
01:08:25,685 --> 01:08:29,104
How can I express the
scale of this loss in a way

728
01:08:29,105 --> 01:08:31,107
that reaches the future?

729
01:08:32,900 --> 01:08:35,278
[waves crashing]

730
01:08:43,494 --> 01:08:47,415
[♪ haunting music playing]

731
01:08:48,833 --> 01:08:51,419
The death of Ok Glacier
is not a single event.

732
01:08:53,713 --> 01:08:56,465
It threatens the life
of everything in our

733
01:08:56,466 --> 01:08:58,009
interconnected world.

734
01:09:00,553 --> 01:09:04,140
Our beaches, our forests,

735
01:09:07,310 --> 01:09:10,772
our seasons, our loved ones.

736
01:09:16,486 --> 01:09:18,196
Our language.

737
01:09:28,498 --> 01:09:31,250
When upheaval happens
on this scale,

738
01:09:33,044 --> 01:09:35,505
only myths feel big
enough to hold it.

739
01:09:38,925 --> 01:09:41,886
In our mythology, the
world is born in ice,

740
01:09:42,220 --> 01:09:46,224
but ends in the ocean,
by way of the sea monsters.

741
01:09:54,023 --> 01:09:56,067
The Serpent of Midgard,

742
01:09:59,821 --> 01:10:01,989
he is the ocean
and the planet itself.

743
01:10:05,410 --> 01:10:08,204
In the Eddas, Thor tries
to conquer the serpent.

744
01:10:15,837 --> 01:10:19,841
But just as he slays him,
the serpent kills Thor.

745
01:10:24,220 --> 01:10:27,264
The waters rise,
and the serpent's venom

746
01:10:27,265 --> 01:10:29,016
infects the seas.

747
01:10:32,145 --> 01:10:35,606
Their mutual deaths trigger
the death of all the gods,

748
01:10:36,816 --> 01:10:39,986
and a total destruction
of everything.

749
01:10:52,623 --> 01:10:55,293
How can I tell the future
the extent of what we have

750
01:10:55,418 --> 01:10:58,838
lost if the future
may not even exist?

751
01:11:02,925 --> 01:11:06,763
[churning waves]

752
01:11:10,600 --> 01:11:14,604
[wind gusting]

753
01:11:29,786 --> 01:11:31,746
[overlapping chatter]

754
01:11:33,706 --> 01:11:36,167
[fireworks exploding]

755
01:11:36,584 --> 01:11:37,752
[Magni] Look at this here.

756
01:11:37,877 --> 01:11:38,920
A little more.

757
01:11:39,879 --> 01:11:40,962
No...

758
01:11:40,963 --> 01:11:42,173
Leave it there!

759
01:11:42,298 --> 01:11:45,051
When this light goes out,

760
01:11:45,176 --> 01:11:46,969
then start it.
Now you can start it.

761
01:11:56,896 --> 01:11:58,523
[Coach] Clear the ball!

762
01:11:59,482 --> 01:12:02,068
[Magni] Yes, well done.

763
01:12:02,193 --> 01:12:03,486
[Coach] And
forward, forward!

764
01:12:07,198 --> 01:12:09,826
[applause and cheering]

765
01:12:12,829 --> 01:12:16,249
[audio rewinding]

766
01:12:22,338 --> 01:12:24,799
[Hulda singing in Icelandic]

767
01:12:34,058 --> 01:12:36,227
[Andri] Let me
send you this song,

768
01:12:36,727 --> 01:12:39,522
a rima that can transport
you through time.

769
01:12:42,400 --> 01:12:45,736
Its patterns of rhyme helped
people to remember long stories

770
01:12:46,404 --> 01:12:48,656
before recording
technology existed.

771
01:12:50,908 --> 01:12:54,245
- There were women
learning strange poems from

772
01:12:54,370 --> 01:12:56,414
their grandmothers.

773
01:12:56,539 --> 01:12:59,125
Who learned it from
their grandmothers,

774
01:12:59,250 --> 01:13:01,251
who in turn learned it
from their grandmothers.

775
01:13:01,252 --> 01:13:04,130
You can feel how time
shrinks down to nothing

776
01:13:04,255 --> 01:13:06,966
when you look at it through
these old women.

777
01:13:08,843 --> 01:13:13,472
[Hulda singing in Icelandic]

778
01:13:23,566 --> 01:13:29,030
[Hulda singing in Icelandic]

779
01:13:43,794 --> 01:13:46,296
[Andri] Rimur transmits
our ancestral stories

780
01:13:46,297 --> 01:13:50,425
across centuries,
each verse a vessel for

781
01:13:50,426 --> 01:13:52,136
keeping the past alive.

782
01:13:57,516 --> 01:14:01,895
Maybe generational memory is
the closest way that humans can

783
01:14:01,896 --> 01:14:04,023
experience the depth of time.

784
01:14:17,745 --> 01:14:22,958
<i>Happy birthday Hulda Filippia</i>

785
01:14:22,959 --> 01:14:26,253
<i>she has a birthday today.</i>

786
01:14:29,840 --> 01:14:31,634
Hi Grandma.

787
01:14:42,228 --> 01:14:44,230
[Hulda] How
was the wedding trip?

788
01:14:44,355 --> 01:14:46,690
- It was incredibly fun

789
01:14:46,691 --> 01:14:48,150
and challenging.

790
01:14:49,068 --> 01:14:52,278
We experienced all kinds
of weather on the glacier.

791
01:14:52,279 --> 01:14:53,364
- Yes.

792
01:14:54,031 --> 01:14:55,616
- We were there
for three weeks.

793
01:14:55,950 --> 01:14:56,992
- Three weeks?

794
01:14:56,993 --> 01:14:58,661
- Yes, on the glacier.

795
01:14:58,786 --> 01:14:59,954
- Wow.

796
01:15:00,079 --> 01:15:01,831
That's a long time.

797
01:15:02,248 --> 01:15:04,417
- It was a fun time.

798
01:15:08,671 --> 01:15:11,132
[Andri] Can you ask
her to calculate for you?

799
01:15:11,590 --> 01:15:13,551
When she'll be 96 years old?

800
01:15:15,678 --> 01:15:17,263
- Do you want to figure out

801
01:15:17,680 --> 01:15:19,974
when you'll be 96 years old?

802
01:15:20,391 --> 01:15:21,517
- Yes, yes.

803
01:15:23,978 --> 01:15:26,563
[Andri] We forgot to
turn on the camera there.

804
01:15:26,564 --> 01:15:28,691
Now, let’s do it once again.

805
01:15:31,444 --> 01:15:32,820
- Andri.

806
01:15:36,240 --> 01:15:37,699
Should I say it?

807
01:15:37,700 --> 01:15:39,409
- Yes, you should ask.

808
01:15:39,410 --> 01:15:41,579
- When will
you be 96 years old?

809
01:15:50,296 --> 01:15:52,756
- 2104.
- 104.

810
01:15:52,757 --> 01:15:53,841
- Yes.

811
01:15:54,341 --> 01:15:57,970
- It will be fun to know
what will have happened by then.

812
01:15:58,345 --> 01:15:59,305
- Yes.

813
01:16:03,559 --> 01:16:05,770
[Andri] 2104.

814
01:16:08,064 --> 01:16:11,900
Looking at it this way, a
future beyond my own life is

815
01:16:11,901 --> 01:16:13,944
beginning to come clear.

816
01:16:17,031 --> 01:16:18,907
It's a place where
my own daughter might

817
01:16:18,908 --> 01:16:21,993
be a grandmother,
and where my own grandmother's

818
01:16:21,994 --> 01:16:24,747
memory can live on.

819
01:16:25,873 --> 01:16:27,874
I can touch time,

820
01:16:27,875 --> 01:16:31,754
stretching back to Grandma
Hulda's birth in 1924,

821
01:16:32,088 --> 01:16:36,801
all the way through 2104,
where we may reach you.

822
01:16:39,095 --> 01:16:42,098
It's like a handshake
of generations.

823
01:16:54,985 --> 01:16:58,280
That was one of the last times
I filmed with Grandma Hulda.

824
01:17:03,202 --> 01:17:06,205
Soon, she too would pass away.

825
01:17:09,208 --> 01:17:11,293
It's a generational
shift in my family.

826
01:17:13,420 --> 01:17:15,130
But from time to time,

827
01:17:15,131 --> 01:17:16,966
I can meet them
all once again.

828
01:17:19,927 --> 01:17:22,470
I can still revisit
the way light reflects off

829
01:17:22,471 --> 01:17:24,098
Grandpa Jon here.

830
01:17:31,522 --> 01:17:33,023
And Arni.

831
01:17:39,864 --> 01:17:41,030
And Disa.

832
01:17:41,031 --> 01:17:42,324
[Andri] Hi Grandma.

833
01:17:49,248 --> 01:17:51,125
[Jon] It's
then that I realized,

834
01:17:51,250 --> 01:17:55,171
it's the same with
people as with flowers.

835
01:17:55,546 --> 01:18:01,010
They wither into puffballs

836
01:18:01,135 --> 01:18:04,262
and spread their seeds.

837
01:18:04,263 --> 01:18:08,851
But from the root,
new life grows.

838
01:18:15,149 --> 01:18:17,318
[Andri] I didn't record
much from their funerals,

839
01:18:18,360 --> 01:18:20,196
only a couple of clips.

840
01:18:21,906 --> 01:18:24,700
[Eilin] ...but I think it's good
for me to see her like this.

841
01:18:33,751 --> 01:18:38,087
[♪ somber music playing]

842
01:18:38,088 --> 01:18:40,925
[waves lapping gently]

843
01:19:00,778 --> 01:19:02,737
[Andri] What jacket
are you wearing, Hlynur?

844
01:19:02,738 --> 01:19:04,447
- Grandpa’s jacket.

845
01:19:04,448 --> 01:19:05,782
[Andri] Yeah, nice.

846
01:19:05,783 --> 01:19:07,618
[Hlynur] The
jacket of the plains.

847
01:19:12,331 --> 01:19:13,666
- Go!

848
01:19:24,802 --> 01:19:27,638
[♪ reflective music playing]

849
01:19:28,264 --> 01:19:31,475
[Andri] I want you to see
how our lives move in rhymes.

850
01:19:36,438 --> 01:19:38,565
Funerals live in sunsets.

851
01:19:43,404 --> 01:19:45,239
Birthdays in sunrises.

852
01:19:52,288 --> 01:19:54,081
Weddings live in
fellow creatures.

853
01:20:01,880 --> 01:20:04,842
Our summer migrations
live in streams.

854
01:20:09,722 --> 01:20:11,307
Harvests in the floodplains.

855
01:20:14,310 --> 01:20:18,814
All this time, nature keeps
records all around us.

856
01:20:21,483 --> 01:20:23,986
In these cycles
of time and water,

857
01:20:25,112 --> 01:20:28,573
I feel a direct connection
to all ancestors and

858
01:20:28,574 --> 01:20:30,159
to all descendants.

859
01:20:51,347 --> 01:20:54,725
But our time is unlike
any time before.

860
01:20:57,728 --> 01:21:03,233
[water trickling]

861
01:21:18,957 --> 01:21:21,502
[wind]

862
01:21:34,181 --> 01:21:38,227
[wind]

863
01:21:42,564 --> 01:21:45,234
[rain falling]

864
01:21:57,413 --> 01:22:01,583
[raindrops]

865
01:22:13,720 --> 01:22:23,230
<i>The singers of the swan
songs are wrestled down by Time.</i>

866
01:22:24,231 --> 01:22:34,867
<i>The new era erases the
footsteps of our rhymes</i>

867
01:22:36,118 --> 01:22:46,753
<i>The only way to keep the verses
of the mother tongue alive...</i>

868
01:22:49,298 --> 01:22:56,555
<i>...is to put this into
the mouths of children</i>

869
01:23:05,731 --> 01:23:07,065
[Andri] Hlynur.

870
01:23:08,317 --> 01:23:11,737
[Andri] This rima that lulled
my son to sleep is an elegy,

871
01:23:14,156 --> 01:23:17,284
a goodbye to the life
the singer held dear.

872
01:23:20,287 --> 01:23:23,707
She urges the listener to
care for what still remains.

873
01:23:33,800 --> 01:23:37,720
Her words of sorrow traveled
into a future that, to her,

874
01:23:37,721 --> 01:23:41,725
seemed foreclosed, but
they still reached me.

875
01:23:59,826 --> 01:24:04,122
[♪ somber music playing]

876
01:24:18,971 --> 01:24:21,806
I had the honor to write
the text on this plaque.

877
01:24:21,807 --> 01:24:25,102
And it's a strange situation
for a writer to write,

878
01:24:25,227 --> 01:24:28,689
in copper, something that
will outlast all paper.

879
01:24:29,064 --> 01:24:31,149
It will even last
longer than this stone.

880
01:24:34,111 --> 01:24:36,445
This is pointing inwards,

881
01:24:36,446 --> 01:24:39,074
and it's also pointing
into the deep future.

882
01:24:39,908 --> 01:24:42,201
And so, people come up here,

883
01:24:42,202 --> 01:24:43,953
and they can
look at the horizon.

884
01:24:43,954 --> 01:24:46,664
But they will also
look at the words,

885
01:24:46,665 --> 01:24:48,749
which we ask here
on this plate,

886
01:24:48,750 --> 01:24:52,254
is that we acknowledge that
we know what is happening.

887
01:24:53,171 --> 01:24:55,591
And we know what
needs to be done,

888
01:24:55,716 --> 01:24:58,175
but only those that will
read the words will know

889
01:24:58,176 --> 01:25:01,721
if we actually stood up
to that challenge that

890
01:25:01,722 --> 01:25:03,140
we were given.

891
01:25:34,463 --> 01:25:35,630
[♪ pensive music playing]

892
01:25:35,631 --> 01:25:38,634
Standing in the place
where the ice once was,

893
01:25:38,759 --> 01:25:40,844
I look across the valley to
the neighboring mountain.

894
01:25:44,556 --> 01:25:48,602
A thick patch of ice,
for now, still a glacier,

895
01:25:49,728 --> 01:25:52,230
is moving slowly down the slope.

896
01:26:28,058 --> 01:26:30,352
[Andri] Hey,
what are you doing?

897
01:26:32,187 --> 01:26:35,065
It’s out of focus.
- I know, very out of focus.

898
01:26:35,482 --> 01:26:37,233
Wasn't it supposed
to be in focus?

899
01:26:37,234 --> 01:26:39,528
It's not focusing.

900
01:26:39,986 --> 01:26:41,405
[Andri] Before Hulda died,

901
01:26:41,530 --> 01:26:43,824
my kids were becoming
the camera people.

902
01:26:46,201 --> 01:26:49,663
We helped Hulda in the
garden many afternoons.

903
01:26:50,205 --> 01:26:51,790
[Hlynur] Here it comes.

904
01:26:57,421 --> 01:26:59,588
[Andri] What do
you think about time?

905
01:26:59,589 --> 01:27:01,424
[Hulda] Time?
- Yes.

906
01:27:01,425 --> 01:27:04,136
[Hulda] Time
passes incredibly fast.

907
01:27:07,431 --> 01:27:10,142
There's always plenty to do.

908
01:27:14,521 --> 01:27:17,149
I never feel alone.

909
01:27:18,567 --> 01:27:20,819
And I often just
start talking...

910
01:27:21,111 --> 01:27:26,366
I feel like he is sitting
in the chair next to me.

911
01:27:28,243 --> 01:27:31,329
We've had a very enjoyable life.

912
01:27:31,663 --> 01:27:34,291
It's good to have the memories.

913
01:27:41,047 --> 01:27:45,010
I start to smell the
glacier in April

914
01:27:45,886 --> 01:27:48,472
and I look forward to going.

915
01:27:49,848 --> 01:27:53,602
But now it's been a
long time since I went.

916
01:27:55,145 --> 01:27:59,316
I would need to go up
with a plane, I think.

917
01:28:16,166 --> 01:28:19,753
[water rushing]

918
01:28:26,176 --> 01:28:29,846
[♪ emotional music playing]

919
01:28:30,222 --> 01:28:32,307
[Andri] I cannot
send you a glacier,

920
01:28:35,602 --> 01:28:37,479
but at least I
can send you this:

921
01:28:42,776 --> 01:28:46,071
A portal to a time when
glaciers are still alive,

922
01:28:47,656 --> 01:28:49,991
held open so they
can continue to be.

923
01:28:53,745 --> 01:28:56,957
I wish in some way I could
know how you received it,

924
01:29:00,710 --> 01:29:03,547
but only time will tell.

925
01:29:12,472 --> 01:29:15,475
[singing rima in Icelandic]

926
01:29:25,986 --> 01:29:30,115
[singing rima in Icelandic]

927
01:29:40,000 --> 01:29:42,711
[singing rima in Icelandic]

928
01:29:56,391 --> 01:30:00,270
{\an8}[singing rima in Icelandic]

929
01:32:36,593 --> 01:32:38,762
[singing rima in Icelandic]